<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John Wesley</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1703">1703</a>–<a href="/years/1791">1791</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John Wesley was an English cleric, theologian and evangelist who led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/methodism">Methodism</a>, a revival movement within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-england">Church of England</a>. Born in Epworth and educated at Christ Church, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a>, he led the Holy Club there and served an unsuccessful ministry in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/savannah">Savannah</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>. At a Moravian meeting in Aldersgate Street, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, on 24 May 1738 he felt his heart strangely warmed, and he then began preaching outdoors and travelling across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a> and Ireland, organising small groups led by unordained itinerant preachers, women and men. Methodists under him opposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>. Barred from many churches and persecuted at first, he was later described as the best-loved man in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-wesley.jpg" alt="John Wesley" width="330" height="402" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: After George Romney · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:John_Wesley_by_George_Romney.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Enlightenment writers such as Montesquieu and Adam Smith attacked slavery as unjust or wasteful, and the evangelical leader <b>John Wesley</b> condemned it in his Thoughts upon Slavery of 1774.</blockquote><a href="/movement/abolitionism">Abolitionism · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>Its words were first printed in 1901 in New Jubilee Songs as Sung by the Fisk Jubilee Singers, published by <b>John Wesley</b> Work Jr. and his brother Frederick J. Work.</blockquote><a href="/songs/wade-in-the-water">Wade in the Water</a></li><li><blockquote>…Myer Lyon, who also sang on the London opera stage as Michael Leoni, sing it at the Great Synagogue of London, and around 1770 wrote &quot;The God of Abraham Praise&quot;, a paraphrase set to the tune now called &quot;Leoni&quot;; <b>John Wesley</b>'s hymnbook printed it, much Christianised, in 1785.</blockquote><a href="/songs/yigdal">Yigdal</a></li><li><blockquote>At a meeting in Aldersgate Street, London, <b>John Wesley</b> experienced an assurance of faith while hearing Luther's preface to Romans read.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-24">24 May in history</a></li></ul></section>
